    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">171 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> starts after acts by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a> lead <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to declare war</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">171 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> wins the Battle of Callinicus and regains most of his losses, including the city of Dion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">168 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus</a> takes command of the Roman force and sends Scipio Nasica on a march round the Macedonian position</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">168 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> withdraws north to a plain near Katerini, south of Pydna, suited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">168 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> break the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> at Pydna and the pursuit lasts until nightfall</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">168 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> later surrenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Paullus</a> and is taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and paraded in triumph in chains</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">167 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Paullus</a> attacks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, enslaving 150,000 Epirotes and sacking 70 cities</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-pydna" data-v="286ac87557" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies stand drawn up</b> The Romans put their two legions in the middle with allied infantry on the flanks and cavalry on the wings. The Macedonian phalanx held the centre, with the Guard on its left, light troops on both flanks and Perseus with the cavalry on the right.</li><li><b>The two centres engage</b> The Macedonians advanced on the Romans a short distance from the Roman camp. The Roman allies tried to beat down the pikes or hack off their points, but with little success.</li><li><b>The allies retreat and the phalanx loses its line</b> Unable to get under the thick bristle of pikes, the Romans used a planned retreat over the rough ground. As the phalanx pushed on into the foothills the ground became uneven and its line lost cohesion.</li><li><b>Paullus sends the legions into the gaps</b> Paullus ordered the legions into the gaps and they attacked the phalangites on their exposed flanks. At close quarters the longer Roman sword and heavier shield prevailed over the lighter Macedonian arms.</li><li><b>The Roman right routs the Macedonian left</b> The Roman right joined the legions after it had routed the Macedonian left. Seeing the battle turn, Perseus fled with the cavalry on the Macedonian right.</li><li><b>The Guard fights to the death</b> The 3,000-strong Guard fought to the death. Nearly 11,000 Macedonians were captured, and the pursuit lasted until nightfall, though the battle itself lasted about an hour.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Perseus of Macedon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Perseus_of_Macedon_coin_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG Coins, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> started in 171 BCE, after a number of acts on the part of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a> incited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to declare war. At first the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> won a number of small victories, largely because Perseus refused to consolidate his armies. By the end of the year the tide had changed: Perseus had won the Battle of Callinicus and regained most of his losses, including the important religious city of Dion. He then established himself in an unassailable position on the river Elpeus, in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p>The next year command of the Roman expeditionary force passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus</a>, an experienced soldier and one of the consuls for the year. To force Perseus from his position, Paullus sent a small force of 8,200 foot and 120 horse under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-cornelius-scipio-nasica-corculum">Publius Cornelius Scipio Nasica Corculum</a> to the coast, as a feint to convince Perseus that he was attempting a flanking manoeuvre by river. Instead, that night Scipio took his force south and over the mountains to the west of both armies, as far as Pythion, and then swung north-east to attack the Macedonians from the rear.</p><p>A Roman deserter reached the Macedonian camp, and Perseus sent a force of 12,000 under Milo to block the approach road. The encounter that followed sent Milo and his men back in disarray towards the main Macedonian army. Perseus then moved north and took up a position near Katerini, a village south of Pydna, on a fairly level plain that suited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had at least 28,600 men and up to 37,000, of whom 22,000 to 34,000 were infantry: Romans, Italians, and allies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, Numidia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a>, and possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. The Macedonians had 43,000 soldiers at the start of the war, of whom more than 20,000 were phalangites. By the time of the battle the Macedonian army numbered closer to 30,000. Before the battle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> had dispatched 8,000 Macedonians (2,000 peltasts, 5,000 phalangites and 1,000 cavalry) to guard against the Roman fleet threatening his rear.</p><p>The cavalry forces were roughly equal: up to 4,000 Macedonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> against some 3,400 Romans and allies. The two armies were drawn up in their usual fashion. The Romans placed their two legions in the middle, with the allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Italian and Greek infantry on their flanks and the cavalry on the wings. The Roman right was supplemented by 22 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> took up the centre of the Macedonian line, with the elite Guard, 3,000 strong, formed to its left. Lighter peltasts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and Thracian infantry guarded the two flanks of the phalanx, and the Macedonian cavalry was most probably arrayed on both flanks. The stronger contingent was on the Macedonian right, where Perseus commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, including his elite Sacred Squadron, and the Thracian Odrysian cavalry were deployed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Paullus</a> had brought Scipio back to the main force, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> deployed for what appeared to be an attack from the south by Scipio. The Roman armies were in fact to the west, and when they advanced they found Perseus fully deployed. Instead of joining battle with troops tired from the march, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> encamped to the west, in the foothills of Mount Olocrus. The night before the battle there was a lunar eclipse. Paullus is said to have understood that eclipses occurred at regular intervals but still thought it necessary to perform sacrifices and wait for favourable omens.</p><p>The fighting began on the afternoon of 22 June. One story is that Paullus waited until late enough in the day for the sun not to be in his troops' eyes and then sent an unbridled horse forward to cause alarm; more likely it began when some Roman foragers came too close and were attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> in Perseus' army. The two centres engaged at about 3 pm, a short distance from the Roman camp. The Roman allies tried to beat down the enemy pikes or hack off their points, with little success, and the Romans used a planned retreat over the rough ground.</p><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> pushed forward into the foothills the ground grew more uneven and the line lost its cohesion. Paullus ordered the legions into the gaps, attacking the phalangites on their exposed flanks, where the longer Roman sword and heavier shield prevailed at close quarters. They were soon joined by the Roman right, which had routed the Macedonian left. Perseus fled with the cavalry on the Macedonian right. The 3,000-strong Guard fought to the death. The battle lasted about an hour, and the pursuit lasted until nightfall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus.jpg" alt="Scipio Aemilianus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_statue_of_a_Hellenistic_prince,_1st_half_of_2nd_century_BC,_found_on_the_Quirinal_in_Rome,_Palazzo_Massimo_alle_Terme,_Rome_(31479801364).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nearly 11,000 Macedonians were captured, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> reported that his various sources claimed up to 20,000 Macedonian dead. Among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Paullus</a>' son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> was thought lost for a while but had been pursuing the retreating Macedonians with some friends, and Marcus Porcius Cato Licinianus, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato the Elder</a>, distinguished himself in a combat in which he first lost and finally recovered his sword.</p><p>The political consequences were severe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> later surrendered to Paullus, was paraded in triumph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in chains and was then imprisoned. The Senate's settlement included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> of many of the king's friends and the imprisonment, later house arrest, of Perseus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-fucens">Alba Fucens</a>. The Macedonian kingdom was dissolved and its government replaced with four republics, which were heavily restricted from intercourse or trade with one another. In time these were also dissolved and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a> became a Roman province.</p><p>In 167 BCE Paullus received orders to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, which resulted in the enslavement of 150,000 Epirotes and the sacking of 70 cities. This happened because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molossians">Molossians</a>, one tribe of the Epirote League, had sent aid to Perseus, but all the Epirotes suffered alike in the Roman attack. The victory was celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where an inscribed decree of the Council and People in 168 BCE honours Calliphanes, an Athenian citizen who had been present with the Roman and Attalid armies at Pydna, for bringing news of the victory to Athens.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Several named writers report parts of the battle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> tells how the Macedonians read the eclipse and that the surviving infantry accused the king and his cavalry of cowardice, having seen the cavalry take no part. Poseidonius claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a> was injured by missiles and brought to the city of Pydna at the start of the battle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> gave the Macedonian dead as up to 20,000 according to his various sources, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Paullus</a> himself later claimed that the sight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> filled him with alarm and amazement.</p><p>The accounts disagree on several points. The exact cause of the start of the fighting is disputed. Other sources state that the cavalry did not take part, because of a strike against Perseus by the nobles, and others that a corps of 10,000 Macedonians was cut off through confusion or a tactical error by the king and did not take part in the engagement. Estimates of both armies' strengths are ranges, and the Macedonian figure fell during the war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle saw the further ascendancy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the Hellenistic world and the end of the Antigonid line of kings, whose power traced back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> Monophthalmus, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>. It was not the final conflict between the two rivals, but it broke the back of Macedonian power. The battle and its political aftermath mark the effective end of Macedonian independence, although formal annexation was still some years away.</p><p>The battle is often considered a victory of the flexibility of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legion</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manipular-legion">manipular</a> system over the rigidity of the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>. The case of the 3,000 Agema peltasts, who kept their cohesion far longer than the regular phalanx, has been taken to show that the training of the troops played an important part in determining both the frontal strength of the pike phalanx and the success of infantry trying to break through the pike wall.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Pydna on 22 June 168 BCE the legions of the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aemilius-paullus-macedonicus">Lucius Aemilius Paullus</a> broke the Macedonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus</a>, who fled. The kingdom was dissolved, Perseus was paraded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in chains, and the Antigonid line of kings ended.</em></p>

